  "RightSideHtmlBlock": "<h4>FIPS 199 adverse effects</h4><p>FIPS 199 explains each impact level through four kinds of adverse effect. Rate each one for this move, and say why in the comments.</p><table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"6\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><th>Potential impact</th><th>Definition</th><th>For example, the loss might</th></tr><tr><td><b>Low</b></td><td>limited adverse effect</td><td>functions performed, effectiveness noticeably reduced; minor damage to assets; minor financial loss; minor harm to individuals</td></tr><tr><td><b>Moderate</b></td><td>serious adverse effect</td><td>functions performed, effectiveness significantly reduced; significant damage; significant financial loss; significant harm, not loss of life or serious life-threatening injury</td></tr><tr><td><b>High</b></td><td>severe or catastrophic adverse effect</td><td>unable to perform one or more primary functions; major damage; major financial loss; severe or catastrophic harm involving loss of life or serious life-threatening injuries</td></tr></table><p><b>Reading the score (high-water mark).</b> Low = 1, Moderate = 10, High = 100, so the total counts the ratings: the hundreds digit is the number rated High, the tens Moderate, the units Low. The overall impact is the highest non-zero digit (e.g. <b>120</b> = one High, two Moderate: overall <b>High</b>). This is how FIPS 199 combines ratings: the highest value, not an average.</p><p><i>Source: FIPS 199, Standards for Security Categorization of Federal Information and Information Systems (NIST, 2004), Section 3 and Table 1.</i></p>",
